# /opt/dripdrop/.env  —  the 171.dripdripdrop.ai instance (Mike's new venture)
#
# Copy to /opt/dripdrop/.env on the new droplet (chmod 600, root-owned). Read by
# BOTH systemd units via EnvironmentFile=. See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-15-whitelabel-instance-design.md
#
# Angle-bracket values are placeholders — every one must be replaced. Nothing
# here is copied from Arena's box except the file's shape: the API key, the
# OAuth apps and the session secret are all this instance's own.
#
# This is NOT a client white-label. This instance is where Mike keeps operating
# after leaving Arena, so every value that points back at Arena is a dependency
# that dies on his last day. That is why none of them may be left at default.

# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A key from Mike's OWN Anthropic organization, on his own card. NOT a key from
# a workspace inside Arena's org: workspace keys are bound to the user who made
# them and are DEACTIVATED the moment that user leaves the organization, so such
# a key would stop working on exactly the day this instance starts mattering.
# Create it with NO expiry, and set a monthly spend limit on the workspace.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<key from Mike's own Anthropic org>

# Session-cookie signing secret. Generate FRESH on the new droplet:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
# Never reuse Arena's — a shared secret means a cookie minted on one instance
# authenticates on the other. The app refuses to start if this is unset.
DRIPDROP_SECRET=<output of: openssl rand -hex 32>

# ── Per-instance identity (the six gates that were hardcoded to Arena) ───────
# Each of these defaults to Arena's value when unset, so every one must be set
# here explicitly. thrivemodal.com is the venture's email domain (partner-owned).

# Who sees the Pipeline (ATS) tab, and who the /ats page admits. The domain
# line already admits every @thrivemodal.com account; the email line is for
# accounts OUTSIDE that domain, which is why Mike's personal gmail is listed
# here. thrivemodal.com is owned by a business PARTNER and Mike will NOT have a
# mailbox on it, so the gmail line is not a placeholder -- it is permanently
# the line that admits him. Add other people by domain, not one by one.
DRIPDROP_ATS_DOMAINS=thrivemodal.com
DRIPDROP_ATS_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Recruiter addresses the résumé contact-extractor must NEVER mistake for the
# candidate's. Verified empirically 2026-09-15: left at the Arena default, a
# résumé sourced by dana.reed@thrivemodal.com returns *the recruiter's* address
# as the candidate's, and that recruiter gets enrolled into candidate outreach.
# Set correctly it returns the candidate. This is the one that actually bites.
#
# NEVER put a consumer domain here. "gmail.com" would match the operator's own
# address -- and also the personal address of most candidates, whose real email
# would then be dropped, silently, with no error and no way to tell afterwards
# which records lost one. Verified 2026-09-16.
DRIPDROP_INTERNAL_DOMAINS=thrivemodal.com

# Same job, matched on the FULL address instead of the domain. This exists for
# exactly the situation this instance is in: an operator working out of a
# consumer mailbox has no domain that can be safely skipped, so the operator is
# named outright. Every address that sources or forwards résumés belongs here.
# Empty by default, so it stays inert on Arena.
DRIPDROP_INTERNAL_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# The Roundup (gated internal newsletter). OWNER is not "who receives it" --
# it is the account whose per-user folder STORES every issue, and the identity
# the send worker runs as (flowdrip_app.py:25375, :26020). So whoever is named
# here, the newsletter sends FROM that account's connected mailbox.
#
# This is Mike's own venture, so unlike a client white-label there is no
# handover: he is the permanent owner and this stays his address. Make it the
# address he intends to KEEP, not a provisioning placeholder -- moving it later
# also moves where issues are STORED, so existing issues must be copied across
# user folders. Cheap now while the folder is empty, annoying once it isn't.
DRIPDROP_ROUNDUP_OWNER=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com
# Who may VIEW the Roundup tab. The owner is always included automatically.
DRIPDROP_ROUNDUP_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Owner of legacy pre-multi-user candidates/pipelines. Inert on this instance
# -- it starts with an empty data dir, so there are no legacy records to
# assign. Set anyway so the value is never Arena's if records ever appear.
DRIPDROP_OWNER_EMAIL=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Not one of the six (it was already env-driven), but it ALSO defaults to
# michael.vaughn@arenastaffing.net -- an address Mike loses on his last day at
# Arena. Unset, nobody on this box has the keys.
DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Permanent admin accounts, checked *in addition to* DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS.
# Was a hardcoded pair of Arena addresses, which made them admins on every
# instance no matter what DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS said. Must be set here.
DRIPDROP_ADMIN_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# ── Outbound identity: what leaves the box ──────────────────────────────────
# This instance's own public origin, no trailing slash. Every absolute URL in
# outgoing mail is built from it: hosted email images, password-reset links,
# newsletter deep links, the API example in onboarding. Left at the Arena
# default, this instance writes image files to its OWN disk and then links them
# on Arena's domain, so every image in this instance's mail 404s. Silently.
DRIPDROP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://171.dripdripdrop.ai

# Company identity stamped on outgoing mail and on the Roundup page. This is the
# legal sending entity under CAN-SPAM, so it must be the venture's real
# registered name -- not a working title.
DRIPDROP_COMPANY_NAME=<the venture's registered company name>

# The CAN-SPAM postal footer on every Roundup issue. This is a legal
# requirement on commercial email and it must be the SENDING entity's own
# registered postal address. The default is Arena's street address -- shipping
# that misidentifies the sender on every issue this venture sends.
DRIPDROP_COMPANY_ADDRESS=<Venture Name | Street | City, ST ZIP US>

# Banner artwork stamped into every J's Way newsletter body. The bundled
# default is Arena-branded. Leave EMPTY to drop the image entirely; set it to
# an absolute URL only once the venture has artwork of its own served from this
# instance.
DRIPDROP_JWAY_BANNER_URL=

# ── OAuth (this instance registers its own Google + Azure apps) ──────────────
# Both redirect URIs default to https://dripdripdrop.ai/... — Arena's host —
# so both MUST be overridden. The callback URLs registered in the Google Cloud
# console and the Azure app registration must match these EXACTLY.
#
# NOT blocked on the partner. The Google scopes this app requests (gmail.send,
# gmail.readonly, openid/email/profile -- gmail_oauth.py:48) are all per-user:
# each person consents for their own mailbox. There is no domain-wide
# delegation and no Workspace admin in the flow, so the Cloud project can be
# created under any Google account, including a personal one, with the consent
# screen set to External.
#
# The catch is publishing status, not ownership. While the app sits in
# "Testing", Google expires issued refresh tokens after ~7 days -- which for a
# scheduled sender means every connected mailbox silently stops sending about
# a week after it is connected. Move the app to "In production" before relying
# on it. gmail.* are restricted scopes, so that transition is where Google's
# verification review actually applies -- budget time for it, not money.
#
# Microsoft is entirely optional: ms_email.is_configured() is False unless both
# MS_CLIENT_ID and MS_CLIENT_SECRET are set. Leave all three MS_* lines alone
# if no Outlook mailbox is being connected.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<Google OAuth client id>
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=<Google OAuth client secret>
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://171.dripdripdrop.ai/auth/google/callback

MS_CLIENT_ID=<Azure app client id>
MS_CLIENT_SECRET=<Azure app client secret>
MS_REDIRECT_URI=https://171.dripdripdrop.ai/auth/microsoft/callback
# Leave UNSET unless the Azure app is registered single-tenant. Unset means the
# MSAL authority is https://login.microsoftonline.com/common, which is what
# Arena has always used and what a multi-tenant registration ("Accounts in any
# organizational directory") requires. Set it to the tenant id ONLY if
# they register the app single-tenant -- /common rejects a single-tenant app
# with AADSTS50194. Until 2026-09-15 this variable was read and then ignored;
# a single-tenant registration would have failed with no obvious cause.
#MS_TENANT_ID=

# ── Registration (NOT optional -- read this) ────────────────────────────────
# Gates account creation. Unset does NOT mean "closed": _load_invite_codes()
# falls back to a hardcoded default code that is the same in every build,
# including Arena's (flowdrip_app.py:2642-2647). Leaving this unset would let
# anyone holding Arena's invite code create an account on this instance.
#
# Set it to a fresh code of this instance's own. Anything unguessable is fine:
#     openssl rand -hex 4
DRIPDROP_INVITE_CODES=<a fresh code for this instance -- do NOT reuse Arena's>

# ── Sales claims (LAUNCH BLOCKER -- read before the first 4×4 send) ───────
# The "proven results" bullet list the 4×4 (and its 5×5 / 5×3 variants) assert
# to every prospect. The default is ARENA'S: an 80-90% fill rate, a 2-3 week
# fill time, and a replacement guarantee. Those are factual claims about a track
# record and about commercial terms. A brand-new venture has neither, so leaving
# this unset means this instance's first sales email makes claims it cannot
# support, under someone else's numbers.
#
# Set it to claims this venture can actually stand behind, or set it EMPTY to
# drop the section entirely. Empty is the honest default until there are numbers.
DRIPDROP_VALUE_PROPS=

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Newsletter stock imagery. Unset just disables the image picker.
#UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=
